    bio: "Sachin Ramesh Tendulkar was born in Mumbai to a middle-class family. His father Ramesh was a novelist and poet, his mother Rajni worked in insurance. As a child, Sachin was a bully \u2014 his older brother Ajit took him to Ramakant Achrekar's cricket coaching clinic to channel his aggression. Under Achrekar's rigorous training, Sachin would practice for hours, often with a coin placed on his stumps that he could keep if he didn't get out.\n\nAt 16, Tendulkar made his Test debut against Pakistan in Karachi, facing the fearsome pace of Wasim Akram and Waqar Younis with a bloodied nose. Over the next 24 years, he would score 100 international centuries \u2014 the most by any batsman in history \u2014 including 51 Test and 49 ODI hundreds. He finished with 34,357 international runs, also a record.\n\nTendulkar carried the expectations of a billion Indians for over two decades. The 1998 Sharjah 'Desert Storm' innings, where he scored back-to-back centuries against Australia during an actual sandstorm, is considered one of the greatest batting performances ever. He was part of India's 2011 World Cup winning team, fulfilling a lifelong dream at his home ground, Wankhede Stadium, where he was carried around the field by his teammates. He was the first active cricketer to receive the Bharat Ratna, India's highest civilian award.",
