    role: "Batsman", era: "Early Masters",    bio: "Donald George Bradman was born in Cootamundra, New South Wales, and grew up in Bowral. As a child, he developed his legendary hand-eye coordination by hitting a golf ball against a corrugated iron water tank with a cricket stump \u2014 for hours on end. This simple practice honed his timing to supernatural levels.\n\nBradman made his Test debut in 1928 and promptly scored a century. In the 1930 Ashes tour of England, he scored 974 runs in five Tests \u2014 still a record for a Test series. His batting average of 99.94 across 52 Tests is the most celebrated statistic in all of sport. He needed just four runs in his final innings to average 100, but was dismissed for a duck.\n\nBradman's record transcends cricket. Statisticians have compared his dominance to being nearly six standard deviations above the mean \u2014 a level of dominance never seen in any major sport. During the Bodyline series of 1932-33, England devised a controversial leg-side bowling strategy specifically to stop him. He still averaged 56 in that series. He was knighted in 1949, the only Australian cricketer so honored while still playing.",
